# an ending
@title The Long Road
@bg field
@mood grief

narrate It worked. That is the first thing, and it must not be buried: it worked. Forty thousand people lived who would not have.
narrate And Corin paid for it alone.
narrate He kept the bones of it — that he had been a soldier, that he had got up in a square at Sallow's Rest. He kept Bryn's face, which was a mercy, because Bryn was still there to sit next to.
narrate He lost the rest. All of it.
narrate And on the last night of the eleventh year, Bryn sat on the bench next to him and said: "Do you want to hear about the bread?"
corin neutral "Yes."
narrate And Bryn told him. The road, and the column, and the boy at the side of it, and the half a loaf, and the words.
bryn grieving "That's the whole of it. That's all I have."
narrate And Corin — who had no memory of the bread and never would — sat on a bench in a garden in the Mire and cried like a man hearing about his own life.
corin grieving "That's the most anyone's ever done."
bryn neutral "It's half a loaf."
corin grieving "It's the most anyone's ever done."

@ending long_road
